# Workbench Showcase

The Workbench showcase demonstrates how to build an IDE-style dockable tabbed layout using JRebirth's **component** module.
It creates a four-quadrant window where each quadrant holds a tab pane, and tabs can be dragged between panes.
The entire layout is defined declaratively through configuration beans — no manual `SplitPane` or `TabPane` wiring required.


## Application Setup

The application class loads two stylesheets: the component module's built-in styles and the application's own customizations.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.workbench;

import javafx.scene.Scene;
import javafx.scene.layout.StackPane;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.Model;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.resources.ComponentStyles;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.application.DefaultApplication;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.workbench.resources.WorkbenchStyles;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.workbench.ui.TabDemoModel;

/**
 * The class <strong>WorkbenchApplication</strong>.
 *
 * @author
 */
public final class WorkbenchApplication extends DefaultApplication<StackPane> {

    /**
     * Application launcher.
     *
     * @param args the command line arguments
     */
    public static void main(final String... args) {
        launch(WorkbenchApplication.class, args);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public Class<? extends Model> firstModelClass() {
        return TabDemoModel.class;
    }

    @Override
    protected void customizeScene(final Scene scene) {
        super.customizeScene(scene);

        addCSS(scene, ComponentStyles.DEFAULT);
        addCSS(scene, WorkbenchStyles.DEFAULT);
    }

}
```

#[[

Notice that `ComponentStyles.DEFAULT` is loaded first — this provides the base CSS for dock panes and tabs.
`WorkbenchStyles.DEFAULT` layers application-specific styling on top.
Both are `StyleSheetItem` constants, resolved from the classpath by JRebirth's resource system.


## TabDemoModel — Declarative Layout

The heart of this showcase is `TabDemoModel`, which builds a complete 4-quadrant layout in a single method using nested configuration beans.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.workbench.ui;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.module.Register;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.ui.ModuleModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.behavior.dockable.data.Dockable;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.ui.beans.DockPaneConfig;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.ui.beans.DockPaneOrientation;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.ui.beans.TabbedPaneConfig;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.ui.beans.TabbedPaneOrientation;
import org.jrebirth.af.component.ui.dock.DockPaneModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.key.Key;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.simple.DefaultSimpleModel;

import javafx.scene.layout.BorderPane;

@Register(value = ModuleModel.class)
public class TabDemoModel extends DefaultSimpleModel<BorderPane> implements ModuleModel {

	private boolean done = false;

	@Override
	protected void initSimpleView() {
		super.initSimpleView();

		final String topLeft = "TopLeft";
		final String topRight = "TopRight";
		final String bottomLeft = "BottomLeft";
		final String bottomRight = "BottomRight";
		final DockPaneModel rootDock = getModel(DockPaneModel.class,
				DockPaneConfig.create()
						.id("RootDock")
						.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.vertical)
						.panes(
								DockPaneConfig.create()
										.id("TopDock")
										.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
										.panes(
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(topLeft+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(topLeft)
																.styleClass("Top Left")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.top)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("1"),
																		dockable("2"),
																		dockable("3"),
																		dockable("4"),
																		dockable("5"),
																		dockable("6")
																		)),
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(topRight+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(topRight)
																.styleClass("Top Right")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.right)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("7"),
																		dockable("8"),
																		dockable("9")
																		))),
								DockPaneConfig.create()
										.id("BottomDock")
										.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
										.panes(
												DockPaneConfig.create()
														.id(bottomLeft+"Dock")
														.orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
														.panes(TabbedPaneConfig.create()
																.id(bottomLeft)
																.styleClass("Bottom Left")
																.orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.bottom)
																.tabs(
																		dockable("15"),
																		dockable("16")
																		)),
												DockPaneConfig.create()
    // ...
```

#[[

### Understanding the configuration tree

The layout is expressed as a tree of three configuration types:

- **`DockPaneConfig`** — splits space horizontally or vertically, containing other dock panes or tabbed panes
- **`TabbedPaneConfig`** — holds a tab strip with dockable content, controlling tab orientation (top, bottom, left, right)
- **`Dockable`** — wraps a model key and a display name to create a single tab

Here is the structure built by `initSimpleView()`:

```
RootDock (vertical)
├── TopDock (horizontal)
│   ├── TopLeftDock → TabbedPane (tabs on top)    [Tab1..Tab6]
│   └── TopRightDock → TabbedPane (tabs on right) [Tab7..Tab9]
└── BottomDock (horizontal)
    ├── BottomLeftDock → TabbedPane (tabs on bottom) [Tab15..Tab16]
    └── BottomRightDock → TabbedPane (tabs on left)  [Tab10..Tab14]
```

### Building the tree step by step

**1. Create a root dock pane with vertical orientation:**

```java
DockPaneConfig.create()
    .id("RootDock")
    .orientation(DockPaneOrientation.vertical)
    .panes(/* child dock panes */)
```

This splits the window into a top half and a bottom half.

**2. Each half is a horizontal dock pane containing two tabbed areas:**

```java
DockPaneConfig.create()
    .id("TopDock")
    .orientation(DockPaneOrientation.horizontal)
    .panes(/* left tabbed pane, right tabbed pane */)
```

**3. Each leaf dock pane contains a `TabbedPaneConfig`:**

```java
TabbedPaneConfig.create()
    .id("TopLeft")
    .styleClass("Top Left")
    .orientation(TabbedPaneOrientation.top)
    .tabs(dockable("1"), dockable("2"), ...)
```

The `orientation` controls where the tab strip appears (top, bottom, left, or right of the content area).
Each quadrant in this demo uses a different orientation to illustrate all four options.

**4. Each tab wraps a model via `Dockable`:**

```java
private Dockable dockable(String modelKey) {
    return Dockable.create()
        .modelKey(Key.create(SimpleModel.class, "Tab" + modelKey))
        .name("Tab" + modelKey);
}
```

The `modelKey` uses a `Key` with a string qualifier, so each tab gets a distinct `SimpleModel` instance identified by its name.

### Retrieving and displaying the layout

After building the configuration tree, a single call retrieves the fully assembled layout:

```java
final DockPaneModel rootDock = getModel(DockPaneModel.class, rootConfig);
node().setCenter(rootDock.node());
```

`DockPaneModel` recursively processes the configuration, creating all nested dock panes, tabbed panes, and tab content models.
The result is a complete, interactive docking layout — all from one declarative description.


## SimpleModel — Lightweight Tab Content

Each tab is backed by a `SimpleModel` — the lightest possible JRebirth model.

]]#


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.workbench.ui;

import javafx.scene.text.Text;

import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.simple.DefaultSimpleObjectModel;

public class SimpleModel extends DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Text, String> {

    @Override
    protected void initSimpleView() {

        node().setText(object());

    }

}
```

#[[

`DefaultSimpleObjectModel<Text, String>` is a model that uses a `Text` node directly (no separate View class) and carries a `String` object.
The `object()` method returns the string qualifier passed through the `Key` — so `Tab1`, `Tab2`, etc. — which is displayed as the tab content.

In a real application, you would replace `SimpleModel` with your actual content models (editors, panels, tool windows) while keeping the same docking infrastructure.


## Key Patterns Summary

| Pattern | Where | Why |
|---------|-------|-----|
| Declarative layout via config beans | `TabDemoModel.initSimpleView()` | Build complex docking layouts without procedural `SplitPane` management |
| `DockPaneConfig` nesting | Layout tree | Recursive horizontal/vertical splitting with arbitrary depth |
| `TabbedPaneConfig` orientations | Four quadrants | Tab strip placement — top, right, bottom, left |
| `Dockable` wrapping | Tab creation | Bind a model key + display name to create draggable tabs |
| `DefaultSimpleObjectModel` | `SimpleModel` | Minimal model for tab content with inline node creation |
| `StyleSheetItem` layering | `WorkbenchApplication` | Combine framework and app styles via `addCSS` |

]]#
